If Next.js is:
Page
 ↓
Hook
 ↓
Service
 ↓
Backend API

then NestJS is typically:
Request
 ↓
Controller
 ↓
Service
 ↓
Prisma
 ↓
Database

Let's say user opens:
/users/123
Next.js sends:
GET /users/123

NestJS receives:
Controller
    │
    ▼
Service
    │
    ▼
Prisma
    │
    ▼
MySQL
Response goes back:
MySQL
    │
    ▼
Prisma
    │
    ▼
Service
    │
    ▼
Controller
    │
    ▼
Next.js

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Typical NestJS Structure
src/
│
├── main.ts
│
├── app.module.ts
│
├── users/
│   ├── users.module.ts
│   ├── users.controller.ts
│   ├── users.service.ts
│   ├── dto/
│   └── entities/
│
├── auth/
│   ├── auth.module.ts
│   ├── auth.controller.ts
│   └── auth.service.ts
│
├── prisma/
│   ├── prisma.module.ts
│   └── prisma.service.ts
│
└── common/

Think:
users/
    ↓
Everything related to users

auth/
    ↓
Everything related to authentication

orders/
    ↓
Everything related to orders

This is called feature-based architecture.


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1. main.ts
Application entry point.

async function bootstrap() {
  const app =
    await NestFactory.create(AppModule);

  await app.listen(3001);
}
bootstrap();

When container starts:
Node
 ↓
main.ts
 ↓
Nest App Created
 ↓
Listening on Port


2. app.module.ts
Root module.

@Module({
  imports: [
    UsersModule,
    AuthModule,
    PrismaModule,
  ],
})
export class AppModule {}

Think:
AppModule
    │
    ├── UsersModule
    ├── AuthModule
    └── PrismaModule
It wires everything together.

3. Controller
Controller receives HTTP requests.

GET /users/123

@Controller('users')
export class UsersController {

  @Get(':id')
  getUser(
    @Param('id') id: string
  ) {
    return this.usersService.getUser(id);
  }
}

Think:
HTTP Request
      ↓
Controller

Controller should be thin.
Its job:
Receive Request
Validate Input
Call Service
Return Response


4. Service
Business logic lives here.

@Injectable()
export class UsersService {

  async getUser(id: string) {
    return this.prisma.user.findUnique({
      where: {
        id: Number(id)
      }
    });
  }
}

Think:
Controller
     ↓
Service

Service decides:
How to fetch user
How to update user
Business rules
Permissions
Calculations

Why Not Put Logic in Controller?
Controller becomes huge.

